<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<assembly manifestVersion="1.0" xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v1">

  <!-- The product is read across monitors of different scaling, and without this it is blurry on
       every one but the first. Microsoft's "Developing a Per-Monitor DPI-Aware WPF Application"
       states WPF apps are system DPI aware by default, and that such an app moved to a monitor of
       a different DPI is scaled by the OS, which "may result in WPF applications to appear blurry,
       especially when the scaling is non-integral".

       PerMonitorV2 stops Windows bitmap-stretching the window and has it tell the window the DPI
       changed instead. WPF then re-renders at the new DPI: "WPF in .NET Framework 4.6.2 enables
       per-monitor awareness", and that support carried into modern .NET. The native helper class
       described on the Windows 8.1 page predates it and is not needed here.

       Both elements are given deliberately. dpiAwareness is read by Windows 10 1607 and later and
       is the only one that can say PerMonitorV2; dpiAware is what older Windows reads, and leaving
       it out would make this DPI-unaware there rather than system-aware, which is worse than what
       the product does today. Where both are understood, dpiAware is ignored. -->
  <application xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v3">
    <windowsSettings>
      <dpiAwareness xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/SMI/2016/WindowsSettings">PerMonitorV2</dpiAwareness>
      <dpiAware xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/SMI/2005/WindowsSettings">true</dpiAware>
    </windowsSettings>
  </application>

</assembly>
